nodes.description:
Observations in which two items (or “objects”) are presented together and one must choose which is preferred (or “wins”) over the other. Each record in the dataset is a triple `(i, j, outcome)` where `i` and `j` are the two items being compared, and `outcome` indicates which item was preferred (for instance, a binary 1/0 if ties aren’t allowed, or 1/0.5/0 if ties are recorded).

Common settings include:
- **Sports**: every game between team A and team B gives one win and one loss;
- **Consumer choice**: a survey respondent chooses their favorite of two brands;
- **Psychophysics**: subjects say which of two stimuli is stronger or clearer.

Statistical models for paired‐comparison data (e.g. Bradley–Terry or Thurstone–Mosteller) estimate each item’s “strength” or “ability” so that the probability item i beats item j is modeled as a function of their strengths.
